  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Saturday...\n\nAn upper low centered over southern WA and troughing over the\neastern US has put the area under shortwave ridging today. Breezy\nsoutheast winds (wind gusts to 20-45 mph) were found over eastern\nareas, while breezy southwest winds (25-50 mph) were found over \nthe southwest mountains and portions of the Paradise Valley. As\nthe low lifts north and east tonight into Monday, an associated \ncold front will sweep across the area. Isolated to scattered \nshowers and a few thunderstorms look to develop ahead of and with \nthe front, though in general, precipitation amounts look to \nremain on the light side. Snow showers are possible over the highest \nelevations, though little in the way of accumulation is expected. There\nremains is a brief window of decent CAPE and shear in our \nsoutheast corner (Carter County) as theta-E streams north into the\narea ahead of the front. Instability is limited, but we could see\na strong storm or two in that area late evening into the early \novernight, and latest CAMs do show a helicity track or two. \nBreezy northwest winds of 20-40 mph will accompany the front with \nshowers potentially aiding some mix down winds bringing locally \nhigher gusts. Hi-res guidance continues to indicate locally \nstronger winds in places with the front (Red Lodge and Sheridan).\n\nBehind the front, Monday will feature dry, cooler and breezy \nconditions. Highs will range from the 60s over the west to the \nlower 70s east. Widespread westerly wind gusts of 30 to 50 mph can\nbe expected, with the greatest wind gusts along the western \nfoothills and out east near the Dakotas border. Locations from Big\nTimber north to Judith Gap have roughly a 40-70% chance at seeing\nwind gusts of 50 mph or greater, with similar probabilities near\nthe Dakotas border. If winds continue to trend higher for the far\neast, a wind highlite may be needed, but will hold off for now. \nAs winds drop and skies clear out Monday night, frost is possible \n(20-40% chance) for some of the colder valley locations with \ntemperatures dropping into the 30s and 40s.\n\nEnsembles continue to prog NW to cyclonic flow will linger behind\nthe departing low, keeping temperatures a little cooler than\nnormal through midweek. Occasional shortwaves may bring a chance \nof light showers to the area at times, Tuesday night/Wednesday and\nlate week. Uncertainty remains high for the latter part of the\nweek, with some ensemble members showing the potential for a more\nwidespread precipitation event, though this is several days out.\n\nHighs are forecast to range from 60s to lower 70s \nthrough Wednesday. Temperatures in the 70s to near 80 degrees are\nprogged for Thursday, with a return to more seasonable\ntemperatures (lower to middle 70s) heading into the weekend. We \nmay see lows in the 30s for Monday night/Tuesday morning, with \nlows other nights mainly in the 40s to lower 50s this week. STP",
